Titre$MERISMODES Earle Henning Knudsen

com$ cf Funga Nordica p 248 / adapt GF mai 2011

1,0 $ On stems of herbs or thin scrubs, rarely on deciduous twigs; hairs very regular, 4.5-6.5 µm broad, smooth on the lower part, finely granulose on the upper part, rather stiff and straight, apex short attenuated, without formation of conidia (fig. 249A). ///Frb c. 1 mm broad and 0.5 mm high, sessile, low cup-shaped, greyish brown. Sp 7.5-9.5(-12) x 5-7(-8)          µm, broadly ellipsoid to ellipsoid or slightly reniform; basidia up to 40 x 7.5 µm, 4-spored. /// Saprotrophic, occurring scattered one by one on last years dry stems of herbs, in the area  found in dry places on Tallacetum, Thalictrum, Orobanche, Artemisiae, Lupinus, Rubus and Ulmus,  in literature reported from many similar hosts; the few records are from summer; rare  in temp. or overlooked; DK (DD), FO. $ M. granulosa (Fuckel) Knudsen, see p. 913
1,0,n$ (Solenia g. Fuckel, Phaeosolenia g. (Fuckel) W.B.  Cooke, Cyphella leochroma Bres., C. gregaria Sydow, C. bresadolae Grelet, C. bresadolae Grelet  var. gregaria (Sydow) PiIM, Merismodes b. (Grelet) Singer)
1,2 $ On wood or bark, rarely on stems of herbs and then hairs different
1,10$ Bibliographie
2,0 $ Sp allantoid, 7.5-11 x 2-3 µm, Q = 3.5-4 (fig. 249B). Otherwise similar to M. anomala. On twigs   and branches of Salix and Betula; winter to summer; common in temp., known from southern  bore; DK (LC), FI (DD, EH) - ®. $ M. confusa (Bres.) D.A. Reid
2,0,n$ (Cyphellopsis c. (Bres.) D.A. Reid)/// The American M. fasciculata (Schwein.) Dank has similar spores but frb like M. stipitata. They may  be conspecific, and then M. fasciculata has priority.


2,3 $ Sp ellipsoid to cylindrical, 8-10 x 3.5-5 µm, Q = c. 2-2.5 (fig. 249C)
3,0 $ Frb short stipitate; stem unbranched and shorter than cup is high, carrying only one cup;  cup c. 0.5 mm in diam., subiculum present between frb and sometimes also outside the area  with frb, but also sometimes almost missing. Sp 8-11 x 3.5-5 µm, ellipsoid to cylindrical,  smooth, sp Q = c. 2-2.5 (fig. 249C); basidia up to 30 x 6 µm, 4-spored; hairs of different types,  thick-walled, brown or hyaline towards apex, smooth on the lower part, the upper part  finely granulose incrusted, usually stronger incrusted at the apex, straight, crosier-formed  or s-shaped at apex, some hairs terminating with a ± regular, balloon-shaped, lageniform or  almost moniliform, hyaline or brown, thick-walled conidium. ///Saprotrophic, forming large  mats or more loose, cm-large aggregations on decorticated wood or bark of deciduous trees,  very rarely on Abies or on large herbs like Epilobillm allgllst(folillm, often occurring on or next ///Spores, marginal hairs and pseudocystidia:  A: Merismodes granulosa,  B: M. confusa, : M. anomala, D:  Woldmaria filicina, E: Fistulina hepatica, F: Schizophyllum commune, G: 5. amplum.  to large, dead pyrenomycetes breaking through the bark; recorded most commonly from  Salix, Fagus and Betula, more rarely from Quercus, Alnus, Ulmus, Carpinus, Pnmus, Populus,  Fraxinus and Rosa; all year round, but possibly more common in winter and spring; common  in temp.-suba.; DK (LC), FI (LC), NO, SE. - B&K 2:224, Ves 126, ®.  $M. anomala (Pers.: Fr.) Singer 
3,0,n $(Solenia a. (Pers.: Fr.) Fuckel, Cyphella a. (Pers.: Fr.) Pat.)  

3,0 $ Frb distinctly stipitate; stem longer than cup, c. 1 mm high, 0.5 mm broad, each stem carrying  one or more cups, cup margins often merged into a coherent mat, when dried the  whole mat cracks up in polygonal fashion at least in the oldest and largest parts; subiculum  present and often well developed;/// frb at first golden brown, but soon becoming whitish  at the apex and darker brown below; hymenium whitish. Sp, basidia and hairs as for M.  anomala./// Saprotrophic on decorticated wood or bark on trunks, stumps, branches and twigs  of deciduous trees and scrubs, mostly on Salix, but also on Fagus, Alnus, Betula, Quercus,  Crataegus and Pyrus; all year round, but possibly more common in the winter and spring;  distribution unknown, probably widespread and occasional. $ M. connivens (P. Karst.) Knudsen, see p. 913 
3,0 ,n$ (Solenia c. P. Karst., Peziza stipata Fuckel non  Huds., Solenia anomala (Pers.: Fr.) var. mali Pilat)   /// This taxon is morphologically distinct when typical, but intermediates exist towards M. anomala,  and it should possibly rather be treated like a variety (var. mali).
